<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Open-Loop Problem</div>
<h2>A standard transformer commits, then can't take it back</h2>
<p>A conventional LLM is <strong>open-loop</strong>. Each hidden state is computed once by a fixed stack of distinct layers and never revised: <code>h&#8202;=&#8202;F_L(&#8202;F_&#123;L-1&#125;(&#8202;…F_1(x)&#8202;)&#8202;)</code>. Three things follow, all bad for reasoning. First, depth is fixed at training time — a 32-layer model is a 32-layer model whether the input is "2+2" or a 12-step logic puzzle. Second, errors made in early layers propagate forward with no chance of correction. Third, the model cannot <em>deliberate</em>: there is no mechanism to revisit a representation, test it, and change its mind.</p>
<p>The original motivation for looping, laid out in <strong>Universal Transformers</strong> <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:1807.03819</span>, was precisely this last failure: standard transformers generalize poorly on algorithmic tasks (copying, sorting, logical inference) once the input exceeds lengths seen in training. Adding <em>more distinct layers</em> doesn't fix it — it just buys a slightly longer one-shot path. What the model needs is the RNN's old trick of <strong>recurrence</strong>: apply the same function many times, refining the state until it's good enough.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">latent trajectory — open-loop vs looped</span><span class="vp-hint">toggle the path</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="intro-canvas" class="viz" style="height:300px"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-controls">
      <button class="active" data-intro="open">open-loop (fixed depth)</button>
      <button data-intro="loop">looped (refine until settled)</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0">Green basin = correct answer. Red basin = plausible-but-wrong attractor. The open-loop path commits to one forward sweep and drifts past the correct basin; the looped path revisits the same weights, spiraling inward until it settles.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout"><strong>The parity canary.</strong> On binary parity — a task where the answer flips with every bit — <em>Closed-Loop Transformers</em> <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:2511.21882</span> show standard transformers approach random performance on hard sequences, while an iterative equilibrium variant gains <strong>+3.28%</strong> on average and <strong>+8.07%</strong> exactly where the baseline collapses. The benefit of deliberation scales with difficulty.</div>
</section>

<section id="s-loop" data-code="loop">
<div class="section-no">02 — Loop the Same Block</div>
<h2>One block of weights, applied S times</h2>
<p>The fix is architectural minimalism. Take a contiguous block of transformer layers and <strong>reuse it</strong>, feeding its output back as its input for <code>S</code> iterations. Everything else — an input "head" and an output "tail" — runs once. The recurrence is the loop:</p>
<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">the looped transformer update</div>
h<sup>(0)</sup> = Head(x) &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># encode once</span><br>
h<sup>(t+1)</sup> = F<sub>θ</sub>( h<sup>(t)</sup> ) &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># same weights, applied S times</span><br>
ŷ = Tail( h<sup>(S)</sup> ) &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># decode once</span><br>
<span class="math-comment"># θ is shared across all t — depth grows, parameters don't.</span>
</div>
<p>This single change does three things at once. It restores the <strong>recurrent inductive bias</strong> of RNNs while keeping transformer parallelism across positions. It makes depth <strong>adaptive</strong> — S can change per input. And it makes the model <strong>Turing-complete</strong> under mild assumptions, where a fixed-depth transformer is not. Universal Transformers proved this and showed a looped shared layer beats a standard transformer on algorithmic and language-understanding tasks, setting SOTA on LAMBADA and +0.9 BLEU on WMT14 En-De.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">architecture — stacked layers vs head / looped-mid / tail</span><span class="vp-hint">compare the two</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg id="arch-svg" viewBox="0 0 720 300" style="width:100%;max-width:720px;display:block;margin:0 auto"></svg>
    <p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0">Left: a standard 8-layer transformer — 8 distinct parameter sets, each used once. Right: head (1 block) → shared mid-block <code>F<sub>θ</sub></code> looped S=6 times → tail (1 block). The looped model has roughly <strong>¼ the parameters</strong> of the depth-matched stack for the same effective depth, because θ is reused rather than replicated.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The same head/mid/tail skeleton recurs across the family. <strong>Hyperloop Transformers</strong> <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:2604.21254</span> (Zeitoun, Torroba-Hennigen, Kim) wrap the middle block with hyper-connections that expand the residual stream into a <em>matrix-valued</em> stream at loop boundaries only — getting competitive quality with <strong>~50% fewer parameters</strong>, surviving post-training quantization. <strong>LoopMDM</strong> <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:2605.26106</span> uses the identical skeleton for masked <em>diffusion</em>: a shared denoising block looped S times over a token grid.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>A looped transformer is a computer.</strong> Giannou et al., <em>Looped Transformers as Programmable Computers</em> <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:2301.13196</span>, showed a <strong>13-layer</strong> transformer placed in a loop can execute general programs — a calculator, a linear-algebra library, even backpropagation in-context — by treating the input sequence as a punchcard of instructions and memory. The loop is the instruction pointer; depth is unbounded; the model is a universal machine. This is the expressivity ceiling every practical looped LLM reaches toward.</div>
</section>

<section id="s-halt" data-code="halt">
<div class="section-no">03 — When Do You Stop?</div>
<h2>The loop count S is the whole game</h2>
<p>Once you loop, one question dominates: <strong>how many times?</strong> Too few and the state hasn't converged; too many and you waste compute — or worse, the iteration diverges. The history of looped LLMs is largely a history of halting strategies. Four have emerged:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fixed S.</strong> Simplest. Loop a constant number of times. <span class="paper-tag">LoopMDM</span> trains with S drawn from U&#123;1,…,S<sub>max</sub>&#125; so the model is robust to any count, then picks a fixed S at inference.</li>
  <li><strong>Adaptive per-position (ACT).</strong> <span class="paper-tag">Universal Transformer</span> adds Adaptive Computation Time: each token independently halts when its own pondering budget is spent, so easy tokens exit early and hard tokens keep looping.</li>
  <li><strong>Fixed-point convergence.</strong> <span class="paper-tag">FPRM</span> (arXiv:2606.18206) and <span class="paper-tag">Equilibrium Transformers</span> iterate until <code>‖h<sup>(t+1)</sup> − h<sup>(t)</sup>‖ &lt; ε</code> — the state stops changing, so the model self-determines depth from input difficulty.</li>
  <li><strong>Budget-conditioned (elastic).</strong> <span class="paper-tag">LoopFormer</span> (arXiv:2602.11451, ICLR 2026) trains on variable-length trajectories with a shortcut-consistency loss, so <em>any</em> loop budget can be chosen at inference and still produce a useful representation.</li>
</ul>

