<p>The empirical payoff of adaptive halting is clean. <strong>LoopMDM</strong> trains with S<sub>max</sub>=12 but adapts at inference to an average of just <strong>S̄=5.1 loops</strong> (ε=0.1) with near-identical downstream accuracy — 41.3 vs 41.4 — a <strong>2.4× compute cut</strong> for free. <strong>FPRM</strong> reports that fixed-point halting lets compute scale with task difficulty across Sudoku, Maze, state-tracking, and ARC-AGI. The lesson: a good looped model doesn't pick S — it <em>converges</em>.</p>
</section>
<section id="s-tax" data-code="tax">
<div class="section-no">04 — The 2026 Renaissance</div>
<h2>A taxonomy of the looped-LLM boom</h2>
<p>Looping went quiet after 2019 — Universal Transformers were expensive to train and the gains on NLP benchmarks were modest. Then 2025–2026 happened. Driven by the test-time-compute era and the realization that modern LLMs are <em>over-parameterized but under-iterated</em>, dozens of papers revived the idea. They cluster into a clean two-axis map: <strong>what gets looped</strong> (y-axis) and <strong>how the loop count is decided</strong> (x-axis).</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">the looped-LLM landscape — click a dot</span><span class="vp-hint">10 papers, 2 axes</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="tax-canvas" class="viz" style="height:440px"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0" id="tax-detail">Click a paper dot to see its mechanism. Y-axis: what is looped — whole shared layer (bottom) → mid-block → frozen pretrained block → latent equilibrium (top). X-axis: how S is decided — fixed → adaptive/ACT → fixed-point → budget-conditioned → training-free.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Four families are worth naming. <strong>(A) Classic weight-shared recurrence</strong> — Universal Transformers, Hyperloop — loops a learned block, chasing parameter efficiency and Turing-completeness. <strong>(B) Equilibrium / fixed-point reasoners</strong> — EqT, FPRM — frame looping as energy minimization and halt on convergence, with stability guaranteed by pre-norm and residual scaling. <strong>(C) Elastic / budget-conditioned</strong> — LoopFormer — decouples train-time and inference-time loop count via shortcut-consistency, so the same model serves a 2-loop quick path and a 12-loop deep path. <strong>(D) Training-free retrofit</strong> — the most practically important, and the subject of the next section.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Expressivity has a ceiling.</strong> Saunshi et al. <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:2502.17416</span> formalize the "k×L ≈ kL" tradeoff: looping a k-layer block L times approximates kL layers, but the recurrent state is fixed-size. A companion result (the <em>memory-budget separation</em>) proves compressed latent loops <strong>cannot</strong> match polynomial-length chain-of-thought on P-complete / logspace problems — a hard theoretical wall that any "loops replace CoT" claim has to reckon with.</div>
</section>

<section id="s-free" data-code="free">
<div class="section-no">05 — Training-Free Looping</div>
<h2>The insight that made looping practical: a block is an Euler step</h2>
<p>For years the blocker was simple: looping only worked if you trained for it from scratch, which nobody with a billion-dollar checkpoint wanted to do. Then <strong>Training-Free Looped Transformers</strong> <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:2605.23872</span> (Chen, Li, Liang, Lao, Liu) observed something elegant. A <em>pre-norm</em> transformer block is exactly one <strong>forward Euler step</strong> of an ODE integrating the residual stream. Naively reapplying the block S times means taking one big Euler step S times — and Euler's method overshoots and diverges when the step is too large. <em>That</em> is why naive looping degrades performance.</p>
<p>The fix is the fix numerical analysis has used for two centuries: <strong>replace one big step with smaller damped sub-steps</strong>. Instead of <code>h ← F<sub>θ</sub>(h)</code>, use <code>h ← h + α·(F<sub>θ</sub>(h) − h)</code> with <code>α &lt; 1</code>. The block's weights are untouched; only the residual blend is damped. This turns a frozen Qwen3 or Moonlight checkpoint into a looped model with <strong>zero training</strong>.</p>

<div class="vp">
