<p>The deeper message: a pretrained transformer is already a looped model in waiting — its blocks are ODE integrators that were only ever allowed one step. <strong>LoopUS</strong> and related post-training recasting work push the same direction: convert a frozen LLM into a recurrent form and recover part of the from-scratch looped benefit. The remaining gap — getting <em>all</em> the benefit without retraining — is the key practical unlock for the field.</p>
</section>
<section id="s-beyond" data-code="beyond">
<div class="section-no">06 — Beyond Autoregression</div>
<h2>The loop principle generalizes to diffusion and decoding</h2>
<p>Looping isn't an autoregressive-only trick — it's a <em>denoising</em> trick, and any model that refines a state can use it. Two of the strongest results in the family live outside standard next-token LLMs.</p>
<h3>LoopMDM: looping masked diffusion</h3>
<p><strong>LoopMDM</strong> <span class="paper-tag">arXiv:2605.26106</span> applies the head/mid/tail skeleton to a masked diffusion language model. The shared mid-block denoises a grid of masked tokens over S loop iterations instead of stacking S times as many distinct layers. The payoff is dramatic: <strong>3.34× fewer training FLOPs</strong> to match baseline NLL on FineWeb-Edu (2.95× on OpenWebText, 2.34× on LM1B), and <strong>+8.5 points on GSM8K</strong> over a 14-layer non-looped MDM. The clearest demonstration is Sudoku, where the loop count directly controls reasoning depth:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">Sudoku — errors collapse as the loop count S grows</span><span class="vp-hint">toggle S</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
